  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381c6e",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "Hepatitis D Virus - HDV IgM",
  "code": "HDV IGM",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>HDV Test Result</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Reactive</div></td><td><div>A reactive HDV test result indicates the presence of antibodies against the Hepatitis D virus. This suggests either a current or past infection with HDV. HDV is a defective virus that requires the presence of Hepatitis B virus (HBV) for its replication. Therefore, a reactive HDV test result can be seen in individuals with an active HDV infection (HDV superinfection) or in those who have had a previous HBV infection and later acquired HDV (HDV/HBV coinfection). Further evaluation, including HBV DNA and liver function tests, may be necessary to determine the stage of the infection and the individual's liver health.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Non-Reactive</div></td><td><div>A non-reactive HDV test result means that no antibodies against the Hepatitis D virus were detected in the blood sample. This result is typically seen in individuals who have not been exposed to HDV or those who have not been infected with HDV in the past. However, in individuals with a known HBV infection, a non-reactive HDV test result indicates the absence of HDV coinfection or superinfection at the time of testing. It is essential to monitor high-risk individuals regularly, as HDV infection can occur later in individuals with chronic HBV infection.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
